        "discipline": "Mechanical",
        "subject": "Mathematics",
        "chapter": "Differential Equations",
        "topic": "General Terminology",
        "subtopic": "Overview",
        "content_html": "<p><strong>Differential Equations</strong> are equations that involve dependent variables and their derivatives with respect to the independent variables.<br />\r\n<strong>Ordinary differential equations</strong> involve only one independent variable., whereas <strong>partial differential equations</strong> involve two or more independent variables.<br /><br/>\r\nThe <strong>order</strong> of a differential equation is the order of the highest order derivative present in the equation.<br />\r\nThe <strong>degree</strong> of a differential equation is the power of the highest order derivative in the equation.<br />\r\ne.g.<span class=\"math display\">\\[\\left ( \\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} \\right )^{3} + \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sin x\\]</span> has order = 2 and degree = 3</p>\r\n\r\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[b_{n}\\frac{d^{n}y(x)}{dx^{n}}+ ....+b_{1}\\frac{dy(x)}{dx}+b_{0}y(x) = f(x)\\]</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p>where <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b_n, ....b_1, b_0\\)</span> are constants are referred as ordinary linear differential equation.<br />\r\nIt’s characteristics polynomial is: <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b_{n}r^{n} + b_{n-1}r^{n-1} + ...+ b_{1}r +b_{0} = 0\\)</span><br />\r\nIf f(x) = 0, it is called <strong>homogeneous equation</strong>, otherwise it is called <strong>non-homogeneous equation</strong>.</p>",
        "important": "",
        "resources": "<div class=\"mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 flex flex-wrap gap-2\"><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1h9e7raTLrLlsMf9uUYGuXMbaZ_c9N7nj/preview\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"px-3 py-1.5 bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-900/40 text-purple-700 dark:text-purple-300 rounded text-xs font-bold hover:bg-purple-200 transition-colors\">👨‍🏫 Tutoring</a><div class=\"w-full mt-2\"><div style=\"padding:74.93% 0 0 0;position:relative;\"><iframe src=\"https://player.vimeo.com/video/580711276?h=e19cb23e95&amp;badge=0&amp;autopause=0&amp;player_id=0&amp;app_id=58479\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"autoplay; fullscreen; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;\" title=\"Degree and Order of Differential Equation\"></iframe></div><script src=\"https://player.vimeo.com/api/player.js\"></script></div></div>",
